      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, it's a technical issue rather than a literal thing. It's just that when I look at those spikes in the residuals, it's almost like the data has its own little agents of chaos that come out to sabotage the trend whenever I get close to a significant result. It's making me feel like the \"noise\" isn't just passive math, but something more disruptive that's actively corrupting the clarity of the model. How do I explain this sense of the noise being disruptive/interfering without sounding like I'm losing my grip on the scientific method?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To describe this disruptive nature without sounding like you've lost your grip, you need to translate your feeling of \"sabotage\" into statistical language. Instead of \"agents of chaos,\" you are observing evidence of *model misspecification* or *non-constant variance*.\n\nWhen you see those spikes, you are seeing clear evidence that the underlying assumptions of your regression—the neat, predictable assumptions—are being violated. You can state that the model exhibits signs of *non-random error structure*, which is a formal way of saying the noise isn't just random static; it has a pattern that is biasing your results. This is a serious methodological finding, not a personal failure.",
